    29	---
    30	
    31	## Background dispatch & liveness protocol
    32	
    33	**The failure mode this kills:** a background-dispatched agent hangs or dies early; the
    34	controller keeps *believing* it is running (sometimes insisting so to the user) until asked
    35	to check the logs — which then show it stopped long ago. Belief is not evidence. The two
    36	rules below are mandatory for every background dispatch.
    37	
    38	### Rule 1 — Observable launch, stall-based judgment, backstop cap
    39	
    40	**Wall-clock time is not evidence of a hang — activity is.** A fixed timeout that kills a
    41	healthy 40-minute run at minute 30 wastes the entire spend: the *stall check* (Rule 2) is
    42	the primary kill criterion, and the wall-clock cap is only a **last-resort backstop** for
    43	when nobody is watching (session ended, controller distracted).
    44	
    45	At launch, three things:
    46	
    47	```bash
    48	LOG=<scratch>/<task>.log
    49	timeout --kill-after=30s <backstop> \
    50	  codex exec … > "$LOG" 2>&1        # backstop ≈ 4–5× the honest estimate — it should
    51	                                     # essentially never fire while Rule 2 checks run
    52	# opencode: same wrapper. agy: --print-timeout <backstop> serves the same role.
    53	```
    54	
    55	1. **Per-task log file** — its mtime/growth is the liveness signal.
    56	2. **Generous backstop cap** (4–5× estimate, or omit entirely for a known-long run you
    57	   will actively monitor). Its job is orphan cleanup, not progress policing — if a
    58	   backstop ever fires on a healthy run, the estimate was wrong: raise it and resume.
    59	3. **Record the session/conversation id** so a kill is never a total loss (see Resume).
    60	
    61	### Resume — a kill is a checkpoint, not a restart
    62	
    63	All three CLIs can continue a killed/expired session; **resume, don't re-dispatch from
    64	scratch** after any backstop kill or hang-kill where partial progress is real:
    65	
    66	| CLI | Resume |
    67	| --- | --- |
    68	| codex | `codex exec resume --last` (or by session id) |
    69	| opencode | `opencode run -c` (last session) / `-s <session-id>` (+ `--fork` to branch) |
    70	| agy | `agy -c` (most recent) / `agy --conversation <id>` |
    71	
    72	Working-tree progress survives the kill too (agents write as they go) — `git diff` before
    73	resuming to see what's already landed.
    74	
    75	### Rule 2 — Evidence-first liveness check
    76	
    77	**"It is still running" may only be claimed after running this check in the current turn.**
    78	Never from memory, never from the fact that no completion notification has arrived.
    79	
    80	```bash
    81	pgrep -fa '[b]in/codex exec|[o]pencode run|[a]gy '   # is the process even alive?
    82	# bracket the first letter: a plain pattern matches YOUR OWN check command (the shell's
    83	# command string contains it) and long-lived `codex app-server` daemons — both false alives
    84	stat -c 'mtime=%y size=%s' "$LOG"; tail -5 "$LOG"   # when did it last say anything?
    85	```
    86	
    87	Verdict table:
    88	
    89	| Process | Log activity | Verdict | Action |
    90	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
    91	| absent | any | **Dead** — regardless of expectations | Read log tail for cause; re-dispatch (once) or escalate tier |
    92	| alive | mtime fresh (< stall threshold) | Running | Leave it alone; re-check at the next threshold |
    93	| alive | mtime stale (> stall threshold) | **Presumed hung** | `kill` it, capture the log, re-dispatch once — second hang ⇒ change model/CLI, don't loop |
    94	
    95	Stall thresholds by CLI (how "silence" maps to "stalled"):
    96	- **codex / opencode** stream incrementally (tool calls, shell output) — log silence
    97	  **> ~5 min** on an active task = stalled **at low/medium effort**. At high/xhigh
    98	  reasoning effort use **600–900s**: a single hard thinking step can legitimately emit
    99	  nothing for over 5 minutes, and a 300s reaper would kill it healthy. Set the wrapper's
   100	  threshold from the effort knob at dispatch time.
   101	- **agy print mode buffers** — the log may legitimately stay empty until the final answer,
   102	  so log-growth is NOT a liveness signal for agy. Rely on `--print-timeout` + process
   103	  existence instead (and remember the brain-file diversion when stdout stays empty at exit).
   104	
   105	### Operating rules
   106	
   107	- **Elapsed wall-clock alone is never grounds to kill** — a slow healthy run and a hung run
   108	  look identical on a clock and completely different in the log. Kill only on stall
   109	  evidence (or let the generous backstop reap a genuinely orphaned process).
   110	- **Check on cadence, not on suspicion**: after launching background dispatches, check
   111	  liveness at the first stall threshold — don't wait for the user to ask.
   112	- **The user asking "is it still running?" is itself evidence it probably isn't.** Run the
   113	  check immediately; never answer from belief. (Track record so far: 3 asks, 3 stalls.)
   114	- **Kill by recorded PID, never by pattern, from any shell that also dispatches.** A
   115	  wrapper shell embeds the dispatch string in its own command line, so even a bracketed
   116	  `pkill -f '[o]pencode run'` kills the wrapper itself (observed 2026-07-22: shell died
   117	  pre-launch, exit 125/144, 0-byte log). Capture `$!`/pgrep the specific pid at dispatch
   118	  time; pattern-kill only from a shell that dispatches nothing.
   119	- **Harness background tasks: use the SELF-REAPING wrapper** (template in the sdd skill):
   120	  the wrapper backgrounds the CLI, records `$!`, and runs the stall-watch loop itself,
   121	  killing the CLI at the silence threshold and only then exiting. This makes the harness
   122	  notification mean "finished or stall-killed", executes the 5-min rule with zero
   123	  controller turns, and leaves the controller responsive to the user throughout.
   124	  Anti-patterns, both observed 2026-07-22: (a) bare `&` + wrapper exit → notification
   125	  fires seconds after launch, long before the CLI finishes; (b) a watcher that only
   126	  *notifies* → detection-to-kill costs extra controller turns; (c) FOREGROUND dispatch of
   127	  anything non-trivial → the controller cannot answer the user mid-call and the stall rule
   128	  cannot fire at all (only the coarse backstop kills; a stalled README probe ran 8 min
   129	  instead of dying at 5).
   130	- On any early exit, the log tail is the diagnosis — silent failure modes already
   131	  documented: agy signed-out (exits quietly), agy `-p` footgun (answers the wrong prompt
   132	  fast), codex stdin hang (waiting on EOF), opencode auth/balance errors (JSON on stdout).
   133	- Record any *new* hang/early-exit signature in this file and append the incident to
   134	  [verification-log.md](verification-log.md).
   135	
