     5	
     6	# SDD with External-CLI Dispatch
     7	
     8	This skill wraps **superpowers:subagent-driven-development**: that skill's process governs
     9	(per-task loop, task-brief/review-package scripts, statuses, two-verdict reviews, fix
    10	loops, ledger, pre-flight scan, final review). This skill replaces its **dispatch
    11	mechanism** with external CLIs.
    12	
    13	Plugin references (read when a step needs detail):
    14	- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/references/sdd-external-dispatch.md` — the playbook + rationale
    15	- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/references/dispatch-reference.md` — verified per-CLI behavior, gotchas, liveness protocol
    16	- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/references/model-catalog.md` — role→tier→model table (the authority for model choice)
    17	
    18	## Step 0 — Setup (once per session, before Task 1)
    19	
    20	1. Invoke `superpowers:subagent-driven-development` and follow its process EXCEPT the
    21	   dispatch steps, which this skill overrides.
    22	2. Run its `scripts/sdd-workspace`; copy the operating contracts into it:
    23	   `cp "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/contracts/"*.md "$WORKSPACE"/`
    24	3. Check the ledger (`$WORKSPACE/progress.md`) — never re-dispatch a completed task.
    25	4. Read the role→model table from `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/references/model-catalog.md`
    26	   and note the routing lever in effect: silent → "floor it" (cheapest model clearing
    27	   each bar); "play it safe" → one tier up on implementers; "via agy" / "delegate
    28	   mechanical to opencode" / "all Claude" reroute lanes. **"all Claude" = use the stock
    29	   skill's Agent-tool dispatch and skip this skill's overrides.**
    30	
    31	## Dispatch overrides (replace the skill's "dispatch subagent" steps)
    32	
    33	**Implementer** (default lane codex; model from the catalog table — ALWAYS explicit):
    34	```bash
    35	BASE=$(git rev-parse HEAD)   # record BEFORE dispatch; never HEAD~1 later
    36	LOG=$WORKSPACE/task-N.log
    37	timeout --kill-after=30s <backstop≈4-5x estimate> \
    38	codex exec -m <model> -C <repo> -s workspace-write -c approval_policy="never" \
    39	  -c model_reasoning_effort=<effort> --skip-git-repo-check -o $WORKSPACE/task-N-status.md \
    40	  "Read $WORKSPACE/implementer-contract.md — your operating contract. \
    41	   Read $WORKSPACE/task-N-brief.md — your complete requirements. \
    42	   Scene: <one line: where this task fits>. \
    43	   Interfaces from prior tasks: <lines, or 'none'>. \
    44	   Write your full report to $WORKSPACE/task-N-report.md. Begin." \
    45	  > "$LOG" 2>&1 &
    46	```
    47	Run in background; apply the **liveness protocol** (stall evidence is the only kill
    48	criterion, never elapsed time — full rules in dispatch-reference.md).
    49	
    50	**Self-reaping dispatch (harness background tasks — the standard shape):** wrap the CLI
    51	and its stall watch in ONE background command, so stalls are killed at threshold without
    52	controller turns, the controller stays free to answer the user, and the completion
    53	notification means "finished or stall-killed":
    54	```bash
    55	<cli dispatch> > "$LOG" 2>&1 &
    56	CLI=$!
    57	while kill -0 $CLI 2>/dev/null; do
    58	  age=$(( $(date +%s) - $(stat -c %Y "$LOG") ))
    59	  [ $age -gt <stall-threshold, 300 for codex/opencode> ] && { kill $CLI; echo "STALL-KILLED after ${age}s log silence"; break; }
    60	  sleep 10
    61	done
    62	wait $CLI 2>/dev/null; echo "cli exit=$?"
    63	```
    64	(For agy, watch process existence + `--print-timeout`, not log age — it buffers and a
    65	log-age watch WOULD kill healthy runs.)
    66	**Threshold scales with reasoning effort** — silence is only stall evidence relative to
    67	the model's expected cadence: 300s at low/medium effort; 600–900s for high/xhigh-effort
    68	dispatches, whose single thinking steps can legitimately exceed 5 min of no output. And a
    69	stall-kill is always a checkpoint, never a loss: session id + working tree survive; if a
    70	killed run turns out to have been healthy (resume shows real progress), resume it and
    71	raise the threshold — never lower it mid-plan on suspicion alone.
    72	Never dispatch foreground for anything longer than a sub-minute probe: a foreground call
    73	blocks the controller from answering the user AND disables the stall rule — the only kill
    74	left is the coarse backstop.
    75	
    76	**Task reviewer** — same shape but **`-s read-only`** (enforced), reviewer-tier model,
    77	prompt names: `task-reviewer-contract.md`, the brief, the report, the review-package
    78	path, and the global constraints copied VERBATIM from the plan. Phrase the restriction
    79	"review only, change nothing in the repo; writing your review file is allowed" — a bare
    80	"modify nothing" makes obedient models skip the review file and answer inline (fine, but
    81	then the verdicts live only in the log tail).
    82	
    83	**Fix / NEEDS_CONTEXT** — never a cold dispatch: resume the implementer's session
    84	(`codex exec resume --last "<answers or findings list>"`; opencode `run -s <id>`;
    85	agy `--conversation <id>`), then re-review.
    86	
    87	## Flavour choice (be explicit which "dispatch" you mean)
    88	
    89	- **Inline** (no dispatch): task below the orchestration floor — a single-file mechanical
    90	  fix the controller finishes in <~2k tokens. Batch several such tasks into one
    91	  ext-dispatch instead of paying per-task cold starts.
    92	- **Ext-dispatch** (this skill's default): Bash → external CLI, per the templates above.
    93	- **Supervised ext-dispatch** (long plans, > ~8 tasks): spawn ONE cheap Claude subagent
    94	  (haiku/sonnet, Agent tool) per task-cycle to run the ext-dispatch templates, liveness,
    95	  mechanical gate, and reviewer dispatch, returning a single report with evidence paths.
    96	  Adjudication and commits STILL happen here in the main thread.
    97	- **Sub-dispatch** (Claude subagent does the work itself) = the "all Claude" lever.
    98	Economics table: references/sdd-external-dispatch.md "Dispatch flavours & economics".
